\section{Related Work and Background}

Modern portfolio selection begins with the mean--variance paradigm \citep{markowitz1952}, which formalizes the trade-off between expected return and variance, and the two-fund separation result in the presence of a risk-free asset \citep{tobin1958}. In practice, however, implementable optimization depends on noisy, time-varying estimates of inputs---especially expected returns and the covariance matrix---\citep{bestgrauer1991,choprziemba1993,merton1980}. A long-standing critique is that the optimizer can become an ``error maximizer,'' producing extreme allocations that are optimal for the estimated inputs but unstable out-of-sample \citep{michaud1989}.

Two themes recur in the literature on making optimized portfolios more stable. The first is to incorporate economically motivated constraints and regularization. Even when constraints are not ``structural truths,'' they can reduce the optimizer's sensitivity and improve realized risk outcomes; long-only and position limits are canonical examples \citep{jagannathanma2003}. The second is to improve estimation. Shrinkage estimators can stabilize covariance matrices \citep{ledoitwolf2003}, and Bayesian/shrinkage approaches to expected returns can reduce the influence of noisy sample means \citep{jorion1986}. A complementary perspective emphasizes that, in many realistic settings, simple diversified allocations can be difficult to beat out-of-sample, providing a useful benchmark for optimization-based strategies \citep{demiguel2009}.

This contribution sits at the intersection of these themes: implementability constraints are kept fixed, and the paper studies whether swapping the risk proxy inside the constraint---from variance-based volatility to robust dispersion measures computed on daily returns---materially changes allocations, turnover, and net performance.

\subsection{Linear and Robust Alternatives to Variance}

Variance penalizes squared deviations and is therefore sensitive to extreme observations. Because financial returns often display skewness, excess kurtosis, and tail events, alternative risk measures have been widely explored. Mean absolute deviation (MAD) replaces squared deviations with absolute deviations, yielding a linear programming formulation with attractive computational properties \citep{konnoyamazaki1991}. Subsequent work studies linear programming-based portfolio optimization more broadly, including robust and downside-oriented formulations \citep{mansini2014}. Empirically, \citet{simaan1997} compares mean--variance and MAD portfolios under estimation risk and finds that, despite their different loss functions, the two approaches tend to produce comparable out-of-sample performance once parameter uncertainty is taken into account---so MAD's computational simplicity is not obviously purchased at the cost of realized outcomes.

Beyond MAD, a large class of robust portfolio methods modifies either the loss/risk functional (to reduce tail sensitivity) or the input estimation (to reduce parameter uncertainty). Minimax-style and related robust formulations can often be expressed in linear or convex programs, offering transparent constraint-driven control of extreme outcomes \citep{young1998}. Quantile-based and tail-focused risk measures, such as value-at-risk and conditional value-at-risk (CVaR), provide another route to robustness by explicitly controlling tail losses; CVaR optimization is convex and has a well-known linear programming representation \citep{rockafellaruryasev2000}. Downside-risk performance measures, such as the Sortino ratio, similarly emphasize asymmetric risk \citep{sortinoprice1994}. This paper focuses on dispersion proxies (MAD, IQR, and a wider central-quantile range labeled ``P95'') that are simple to interpret and straightforward to compute on rolling windows; these measures are not intended to be the last word on tail risk, but rather a practical set of alternatives that can be embedded into a constrained, implementable optimizer.

\subsection{Turnover, Rebalancing Frequency, and Transaction Costs}

Rebalancing can improve risk control and allow the investor to respond to changing return and covariance structure, but it mechanically induces turnover. Turnover matters because trading is costly: even in liquid markets, the cost of active investing can materially erode performance \citep{french2008}. Classic theory makes this point sharply: with transaction costs, optimal policies generally involve a trade-off between tracking the desired target and avoiding excessive trading, often generating ``no-trade'' regions and gradual adjustment rather than continuous re-optimization \citep{constantinides1986}.

From an implementation perspective, costs are not only explicit fees, but also spreads and market impact. The market microstructure literature formalizes how trading moves prices and how liquidity provision affects execution costs \citep{kyle1985}, while optimal execution models characterize the trade-off between impact and timing when trading a target position \citep{almgrenchriss2001}. More recent dynamic portfolio choice work emphasizes that predictable returns can justify trading, but that transaction costs and risk constraints fundamentally shape optimal turnover \citep{garleanupedersen2013}. These impact- and execution-aware models are considerably more realistic than a proportional-cost assumption, but they also introduce additional parameters (e.g., impact coefficients, liquidity dynamics) that are themselves estimated with error and are difficult to calibrate consistently across nine sector ETFs, nineteen rebalancing/risk-budget combinations, and 25 years of data. In this sector-ETF setting, the paper instead uses a simple proportional cost model applied to turnover as a deliberately transparent proxy for these frictions, prioritizing comparability across the many risk-proxy and rebalancing-schedule combinations tested over the added realism of a full market-impact model, and evaluates whether risk-proxy differences translate into economically meaningful turnover differences.

\subsection{Backtest Integrity}

Because optimization and rebalancing are sensitive to the information set, careful backtest design is essential. The backtest follows a strict no-look-ahead timeline: weights for a holding period are computed using only returns from the immediately preceding estimation window (Figure 2). This design choice ensures that the performance evaluation reflects an implementable process rather than inadvertent leakage of future information.

More broadly, the backtesting literature cautions that repeated searches over strategies, hyperparameters, and sample splits can lead to data snooping and selection bias, in which apparently strong in-sample results fail out of sample. A foundational reference is the ``reality check'' approach, which explicitly accounts for multiple testing when evaluating strategy performance \citep{white2000}. In addition, practical guidance emphasizes guarding against backtest overfitting and selection bias when comparing many strategy variants \citep{lopezdeprado2018}. This paper does not attempt a formal multiple-testing correction such as White's reality check; instead, it limits the scope for data snooping structurally, by fixing a small, pre-specified, fully crossed design---four risk proxies, three annualized risk budgets, and four rebalancing schedules---rather than searching freely over model variants, hyperparameters, or sample splits. Combined with fixed rules, explicit no-look-ahead timelines, and reported turnover/cost sensitivity, this transparency-first design is intended to reduce the scope for inadvertent overfitting, even without a formal statistical correction.

\subsection{Summary and Gap}

Taken together, prior work has largely studied these themes in isolation. Robust dispersion and quantile-based risk measures have mostly been evaluated against mean--variance either without the joint implementability constraints (long-only, fully invested, allocation caps, and an explicit cash sleeve) used here, or without systematically varying rebalancing frequency alongside the risk proxy. Conversely, the rebalancing and transaction-cost literature typically studies how often to trade a fixed target allocation, largely independent of how that target is chosen; recent empirical work on ETF portfolios, for instance, examines how rebalancing affects realized risk-adjusted returns without varying the underlying risk proxy used to set target weights \citep{banyai2024}. This paper's contribution is to jointly vary the risk proxy and the rebalancing schedule under a single, implementable set of constraints and a strict no-look-ahead backtest, which is what allows it to compare the economic magnitude of these two design choices directly rather than studying either in isolation.

